<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Nasir ibn Alnas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1088</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Nasir ibn Alnas was the Hammadid ruler of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> from 1062 to 1088, under whom the dynasty reached its height. Pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> Arab tribes in the interior, he founded a new capital on the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a>, which he called al-Nasiriyya, and developed it as a port trading with Italian cities. He also exchanged letters with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>.</p></div>
